description:
Raised by decision 0011, Questions for the project owner #1. Three things currently in the repo cannot all hold:

- the volunteer mockup asks for 'no user accounts / phone app needed' and 'can be accessed from anywhere';
- volunteers are a pick-list with no password (decision 0008 point 5);
- the backend's DEFAULT_PERMISSION_CLASSES is IsAuthenticated.

Someone has to decide whether the batch endpoint is reachable by anyone who can reach the site, whether the site sits behind something at the network or proxy layer, or whether a single shared credential is issued. The QR flow design works unchanged under any of these - the question is who NYC Mesh is willing to let write to its ledger, which is not a decision for an implementer.

Does NOT block implementation: the endpoints are built with the project default and relaxing it is one line. It DOES need answering before volunteers use the system.

Found by the simplify pass on PR #36, and its own issue because no single issue in that batch owns all the call sites -- the third row of DEVELOPERS.md's triage table.

THE DUPLICATION. Five components in frontend/src/admin/ now carry the same twelve lines: a `saving` and a `refused` state pair; the frame

    setSaving(true); setRefused(null);
    try { ...the one line that differs...; onSaved(); onClose(); }
    catch (error) { setRefused(asApiError(error)); }
    finally { setSaving(false); }

and the `{refused ? <Refusal error={refused} onDismiss={() => setRefused(null)} /> : null}` beside it. EditItem.tsx and CreateItem.tsx are inventory-tng-2oba.1's, EditCategory.tsx and EditLocation.tsx are inventory-tng-2oba.2's, RevokeLabel.tsx is inventory-tng-2oba.3's.

AND IT HAS ALREADY DRIFTED, which is what makes this worth doing rather than noting. RevokeLabel clears its own `asking` state inside the try rather than calling an onClose, so the one component whose success path differs is the one that had to retype the frame to say so. The error path is the half nobody re-reads, and it is now written five times.

WHAT TO DO. A `useSaving()` in frontend/src/admin/ returning { saving, refusal, run }, where `run` takes the async body and owns the set, the clear, the catch and the finally, and `refusal` is the rendered <Refusal> or null. Each caller keeps only its own body and its own success tail, so nothing has to be parameterised and the five components stay as different from each other as they are.

WHAT TO PIN. Nothing new: all five already have tests covering their refusal, including the two kinds of 403. The extraction is done when those pass unchanged, which is the point of doing it while they are all green.

loadDetector() in frontend/src/scan/decoder.ts memoises the promise rather than its result:

    detector ??= (async () => { await import("barcode-detector/pure"); ... })();

A single failed dynamic import -- a flaky connection in a basement, or a stale index.html after a deploy pointing at a chunk that is no longer served -- leaves `detector` holding a permanently rejected promise. Every later call answers with the same rejection: after Stop and Start, after a lens switch, after the volunteer closes and reopens the scanner. Nothing short of reloading the page clears it, because forgetDetector() exists only for tests and its own comment says so.

What the volunteer sees, now that CameraScanner releases the stream when the open fails (inventory-tng-w1e): the camera opens, the warning appears, and every retry fails identically for the life of the tab.

APPROACH

Clear the memo when the load rejects, so the next open retries and a successful load is still kept:

    detector ??= load().catch((error: unknown) => {
      detector = null;
      throw error;
    });

Worth checking at the same time whether the failure reaches the volunteer as something better than refusal()'s fallback -- 'the camera could not be opened' is not what happened, and 'the scanner could not be downloaded; try again' names the way out.

Raised by the xhigh review of inventory-tng-w1e, which found it while reviewing the code that now depends on the retry working.

Found by the simplify pass on PR #31 (batch/an-amend-is-not-a-new-commit), and deliberately not fixed there: eighteen of the twenty predate that batch, so it is duplication the batch landed beside rather than duplication it caused, and rewriting half a suite inside a fixup would have made the review diff unreadable.

scripts/check-commit.test.sh writes this heredoc twenty times, byte for byte:

  tracker <<'JSONL'
  {"_type":"issue","id":"inventory-tng-aaa","title":"one","status":"closed"}
  {"_type":"issue","id":"inventory-tng-bbb","title":"two","status":"in_progress"}
  {"_type":"issue","id":"inventory-tng-old","title":"done long ago","status":"closed"}
  JSONL

Sixty lines of identical JSON. A helper beside good_message -- closes_aaa, say -- collapses it, and the handful of cases that vary a row keep their own heredoc so the difference is what the reader sees rather than something to diff for.

Worth doing carefully rather than mechanically: the variants are near-identical and a wrong substitution turns a case green for the wrong reason. Check each replacement against the block it replaces, and the suite count (64 at the time of writing) must not move.
